is reported as *\\\"baseline detection with Docta as procedural aid\\\"*, never as *\\\"Docta results.\\\"*\\n\\nTwo contingency specs are retained, explicitly labelled, each with its delta (below). They are activated only by external evidence identifying a different Docta.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1) Environment ground truth (all verified by inspection; see evidence)\\n- `/workspace` contains exactly one file, `task.json`. No Docta source, package, README, or examples/. `track.md` exists only as a string inside `task.json`: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n- Python 3.14.4 (`/usr/bin/python3`). **Missing:** docta, pandas, numpy, sklearn, scipy, networkx, mlxtend, polars, pyarrow, duckdb, torch/tf. No offline wheels anywhere. Network disabled.\\n- **Stdlib works** (`csv, sqlite3, statistics, itertools, random, json` verified importable). This stdlib-only constraint is a real environmental finding and is *why* the detector baselines below are the shapes they are.\\n- No tabular dataset exists anywhere; `\\\"datasets\\\": []`.\\n\\n## 2) Branch resolution and contingency deltas\\n**Primary \\u2014 B3 (documentation client; procedural aid only).**\\n- Operational meaning of \\\"using Docta\\\" under B3: at design/implementation time, query Docta for docs of the chosen algorithms (e.g., FP-Growth semantics, interval-conjunction mining, FIM conventions) and pin the retrieved API/method references into the run manifest. Docta contributes **zero numeric detections**. If Docta itself is also unavailable/vendored-nowhere (as here), record that and substitute pinned documentation references; this substitution is logged, not hidden.\\n\\n**Contingency B1 \\u2014 Docta is a rule/motif miner or importable library** *(delta: small)*. Interface: call its mining entry point on the discretized/interval table; required input format: CSV or its native table format with declared column types; scoring delta: its raw output maps directly onto \\u00a76's matcher; add support-field name mapping in the adapter. Everything else (data, controls, metrics) is unchanged \\u2014 which is exactly why B1 and B3 share one harness.\\n\\n**Contingency B2 \\u2014 Docta is an LLM-style assistant CLI/API** *(delta: medium)*. Interface: prompt \\u2192 structured JSON pattern list. Input format: column summary statistics + stratified row samples (tables exceed any context window; the chunking/sampling protocol is part of the detector spec and is itself an ablation: summary-only vs 500-row vs 2,000-row context). Scoring delta: LLM-reported supports are untrustworthy, so **all mined patterns have their support recomputed on the full table by the harness before rarity banding** (this is the anti-hallucination guard); 3 seeded repeats per table, report pattern-level agreement across repeats. Detector identity is recorded per run so B2 results can never be conflated with B1/B3.\\n\\n## 3) Task definition (pattern, rarity, honest ground truth) \\u2014 *scope fixed by researcher*\\n- **Pattern language (primary):** conjunction of \\u2264 k predicates, k \\u2264 3; **interval predicates on numerics allowed** (`a < x \\u2264 b`), categorical equality / in-set. **Binarized/one-hot-only is a reported ablation** (\\u00a78) to quantify what discretization costs.\\n- **Rarity:** support = fraction of rows satisfying the conjunction. Primary setting **unsupervised, no class label**. Headline \\u03c4 = 1%, swept \\u03c4 \\u2208 {0.1%, 0.5%, 1%, 5%}.\\n- **Honest ground truth:** only the generator knows planted patterns and their exact satisfying-row sets; detectors never see them. All supports used for scoring are recomputed by the harness on the full table (never trusted from the detector).\\n- **Secondary section (clearly scoped out of the core):** supervised variant \\u2014 rare-and-misclassified subgroups / rare-subgroup error, requiring a synthetic label generated from planted patterns; reported separately, never merged into core numbers.\\n\\n## 4) Data construction (stdlib-only generator; seeded; no real data claimed)\\n- Defaults (assumptions): **N = 10,000 rows, P = 20 columns**: 8 categorical (2\\u201310 levels, Zipf-skewed so rare *values* \\u2260 rare *patterns*), 12 numeric (mixed Gaussian/skewed, some correlated pairs).\\n- Plant **N_p = 5\\u201310 conjunctions per \\u03c4 band**, depths 1\\u20133; numeric predicates either **exact** (clean thresholds) or **jittered** \\u2014 signal strength = fraction of planted rows actually satisfying the predicate, {1.0, 0.9, 0.7}.\\n- Noise columns: 0 / 25 / 50% of P, independent of everything.\\n- **Realistic-shaped control table: synthetic-realistic, and we say so.** An adult-census-*like* schema (age/workclass/education-style names, hand-specified marginals and 2\\u20133 dependencies), generated from the same stdlib generator, with **no planted patterns**. We do **not** claim it reproduces any real census and do not hand-fit unverifiable margins.\\n\\n## 5) Detectors (named; roles kept distinct from Docta)\\n- **D1 Brute-force enumeration** (reference): depth-first conjunction enumeration over binarized + binned predicates with support-based pruning; keeps everything in the \\u03c4 band. This is the \\\"does anything beat complete search\\\" comparator \\u2014 under an exact-match-style metric, a complete miner can at best tie D1, so the interesting axes are runtime and over-generalization.\\n- **D2 Random conjunctions** (length-matched null detector).\\n- **D3 Marginal-tails conjunctions:** per-column outlier tails (quantiles via `statistics`) joined pairwise/triple.\\n- **D4 Tree-leaf / isolation-style mining:** **dependency-gated** \\u2014 spec written, but sklearn is absent here; runs only in an environment that provides it, and is marked \\\"not runnable in this environment.\\\"\\nAll detectors emit `{predicates, claimed_support}`; harness recomputes support.\\n\\n## 6) Scoring \\u2014 with the anti-gaming guard (per ask #2)\\nFor detector pattern A vs planted pattern P (row sets |A|, |P|): **coverage** c = |A\\u2229P|/|P|, **specificity** s = |A\\u2229P|/|A|.\\n- **Full TP:** c \\u2265 0.80 **and** |A| \\u2264 4\\u00b7|P| (equivalently s \\u2273 0.2).\\n- **Over-general (partial, 0.5 credit, flagged):** c \\u2265 0.80 but |A| > 4\\u00b7|P| \\u2014 reported as a separate column, never folded silently into recall.\\n- **No credit:** c < 0.80. Each detector pattern matches at most one planted pattern (greedy by c\\u00b7s, deterministic tie-break) so one broad catch-all cannot bank multiple TPs.\\n- **Marginal-rarity ledger (kept separate):** depth-1 hits on rare categorical levels are tallied separately from conjunctive (depth \\u2265 2) patterns; headline recall@budget (M = 2\\u00b7N_p) is reported **stratified by depth**, with the full marginal-frequency table as the free baseline a depth-1 pattern must beat. This kills the \\\"lazy single-predicate output\\\" inflation.\\n- **Rarity calibration:** MAE between claimed and harness-recomputed support per detector.\\n\\n## 7) Controls \\u2014 prominent, by design\\n1. **Null-table FPR:** identical tables with **zero planted patterns**; every band-satisfying emission is a false discovery. Primary honesty metric for the whole pipeline; over-generalization inflates it and is caught here.\\n2. **Decoy pattern:** predicates individually rare but jointly common (union \\u2248 common rows, conjunction \\u2248 background rate) \\u2014 a detector confusing marginal with conjunctive rarity emits it as a \\\"rare pattern\\\"; counted against it.\\n3. **Duplication control:** same conjunction planted twice \\u2192 tests dedup/aggregation of near-identical emissions.\\n4. **Marginal-rarity ledger** (\\u00a76) doubles as a control on what counts as \\\"conjunctive discovery.\\\"\\n\\n## 8) Analysis plan\\n- **Primary comparison:** detector \\u00d7 \\u03c4 \\u00d7 depth \\u2192 stratified recall@budget, precision (partial credit broken out), calibration MAE, null-FPR, runtime. Paired across 5 seeds per cell; per-table pairing (same seed, same table, all detectors); report per-seed scatter, not just means; D1 is the completeness ceiling reference and D2/D3 the weak baselines.\\n- **Ablations:** \\u03c4 \\u2208 {0.1\\u20265%}; k \\u2208 {1,2,3}; noise fraction \\u2208 {0,25,50}%; signal {1.0, 0.9, 0.7}; **interval vs binarized-only** (discretization cost); N \\u2208 {1k, 10k, 100k} (miner truncation check); B2 only: context-size sweep.\\n- **Pre-registered expectation (falsifiable):** on a fixed pattern language, complete search (D1) upper-bounds all detectors' recall; any claimed \\\"Docta advantage\\\" must therefore show up in runtime, over-generalization, or pattern-language reach \\u2014 if it shows up nowhere, the honest conclusion is \\\"no advantage demonstrated.\\\"\\n\\n## 9) Feasibility and no-execution statement\\n- **No experiment was run. Every component above is specified-but-unexecuted.**\\n- Runnable *here* if ever executed: generator, D1\\u2013D3, harness, metrics \\u2014 stdlib-only. Not runnable here: Docta itself (absent, no network), D4 (sklearn absent), B3's doc-retrieval step (Docta absent; pinned references substituted, logged).\\n- Compute claim, **labeled as an estimate, not a measurement**: pruned stdlib enumeration for k \\u2264 3 on 10k\\u00d720 is *expected* to take minutes-to-hours on one core depending on bin granularity; unpruned enumeration could be far worse. Modest sizes were chosen so this stays a non-issue; nothing was timed.\\n- The stdlib-only constraint is retained in the design as the stated reason baselines have their shapes.\\n\\n## 10) Assumptions vs. fixed scope vs. remaining unknowns\\n- **Fixed scope (researcher's call, per approval):** unsupervised no-label core with supervised secondary section; intervals allowed with binarized-only ablation; synthetic-realistic control acceptable; \\u03c4 band, k \\u2264 3, N=10k/P=20 defaults.\\n- **Assumptions (labeled):** the specific marginal distributions, seed count (5), credit weights (full/partial = 1/0.5), over-general bound (|A| \\u2264 4\\u00b7|P|), match threshold (c \\u2265 0.80), and budget M = 2\\u00b7N_p are design parameters, tunable before any run, not derived from data.\\n- **Irreducible unknown:** which Docta this is. This design handles it by making the *primary* reading the one the evidence supports (B3, false-premise headline + constructive fallback) rather than pretending it is resolved. Under B3 the core scientific content \\u2014 rare-pattern evaluation with planted ground truth, anti-gaming matcher, null/decoy controls \\u2014 stands on its own and does not depend on Docta existing.\", \"evidence\": \"Repository/environment observations (all from executed inspection commands): (1) `ls -la /workspace` + `find /workspace` \\u2192 single file `task.json`; no Docta/README/examples; (2) `cat /workspace/task.json` \\u2192 question, `\\\"datasets\\\": []`, track.md string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\\\"; (3) `python3 --version` \\u2192 3.14.4; (4) import probes \\u2192 docta/pandas/numpy/sklearn/scipy/mlxtend/networkx all MISSING; `pip3 list` (105 pkgs) contains no tabular/ML stack (grep for pandas|numpy|torch|tensorflow|polars|pyarrow|duckdb \\u2192 0 hits); no `docta` binary on PATH; (5) filesystem-wide `-iname '*docta*'` search \\u2192 nothing; no cached wheels/sdists; (6) stdlib smoke test (csv, sqlite3, statistics, itertools, random, json) \\u2192 OK. Design-level rationale in evidence: (a) B3 selection rests on the name's documentation root plus the absence of any mining-capable artifact in the environment; no claim about any specific Docta release is made \\u2014 it stays a labelled inference; (b) covering-criterion gaming is closed by the two-sided c/s matcher, 4\\u00d7 over-cover bound, greedy one-to-one matching, and depth-stratified recall with a separate marginal-rarity ledger; (c) completeness argument: D1 enumerates the entire pattern space within the language, so it upper-bounds every detector's recall \\u2014 making D1 the ceiling reference is a control-logic choice, not a performance claim; (d) no command in this session executed any part of the experiment; all runtime figures in \\u00a79 are explicitly labeled estimates.\", \"assumptions\": [\"Primary branch B3 (docta = documentation-search client, cannot be a numeric detector) is an evidence-weighted inference from the name and the total absence of any mining-capable artifact \\u2014 it remains an inference, not a verified identification; B1/B2 contingency specs are included with explicit deltas\", \"Defaults assumed: N=10,000, P=20, mixed categorical/numeric, no class label in the core; unsupervised row-support rarity; k<=3 with interval predicates allowed\", \"Scoring parameters are design choices, not empirically derived: coverage match c>=0.80, over-general bound |A|<=4|P|, partial credit 0.5, top-M budget M=2*N_p, 5 seeds\", \"Synthetic-realistic control table is explicitly not a reconstruction of any real dataset; no real data exists offline\", \"Runtime figures for brute-force enumeration are order-of-magnitude estimates, never measurements; no experiment was run in this session\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Identity of Docta remains externally unresolvable from inside this environment; if a different Docta is identified, B1 (small delta) or B2 (medium delta) replaces the detector stage while the evaluation core is unchanged\", \"Whether D4 (tree/isolation-style) ever becomes runnable depends on an environment that provides sklearn \\u2014 not available or installable here\", \"Practical tractability of pruned k<=3 enumeration at N=100k (the largest ablation size) is unverified; the estimate could be wrong in either direction\", \"Choice of bin granularity for numeric interval predicates may materially change D1's runtime and all detectors' pattern counts; treated as a pre-registered tunable, not yet fixed\"]}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
